//! # Structured concurrency
//!
//! Ordinary calls wait. `fork` creates a `Task<T>` and `await task` yields `T`.
//! Awaiting a `Vec<Task<T>>` consumes it and yields `Vec<T>` in vector order.
//! If `T` is a Result, each application error remains a value to handle.
//!
//! A scope owns its child tasks and produces a value after their cleanup. A plain
//! call in a scope is still a call; it does not implicitly start a task.
//!
//! ```hew
//! fn left() -> i64 { 20 }
//! fn right() -> i64 { 22 }
//! fn main() {
//!     let total = scope {
//!         let a = fork left();
//!         let b = fork right();
//!         (await a) + (await b)
//!     };
//!     println(total);
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! `await fork [left(), right()]` collects a vector; the parenthesized batch
//! form collects a tuple. Each batch expression first creates a task.
//!
//! `scope within duration { ... } handle failure { ... }` bounds structured work.
//! A fault or cancellation settles children and cleanup before recovery; an
//! ordinary Err does not trigger it. Parent cancellation continues outward.
//! Cancellation is cooperative and cannot skip resource cleanup.
//!
//! `race` chooses the first completion, including an ordinary Err, and cleans up
//! losers. `select` uses `value from source => ...` and `after duration => ...`.
//! Task/timer selection is on the native path; actor/channel registration remains
//! pending at this checkpoint. Actor streams use ordinary `for`, without await.
